    "explanation": "A Incorrect as the quick ratio is calculated as (cash + short-term marketable investments + receivables)/current liabilities. Neither the numerator nor the denominator is affected by an inventory write-down as any write-down to market value or net realizable value reduces the value of inventory, and the loss in value (expense) is generally reflected in the income statement in cost of goods sold. Therefore, the quick ratio is not affected by an inventory write-down.\nB Incorrect as the current ratio is calculated as Current assets/Current liabilities and an inventory write-down will affect the numerator (since inventory is a current asset) and will have no effect on the denominator since any write-down to market value or net realizable value reduces the value of inventory, and the loss in value (expense) is generally reflected in the income statement in cost of goods sold. Therefore, the current ratio will be lower after the write-down than it was prior to the write-down and a lower ratio indicates less liquidity, implying a greater reliance on operating cash flow and outside financing to meet short-term obligations.\nC Correct because the working capital turnover ratio is calculated as Revenue/Average working capital. Working capital is defined as current assets minus current liabilities. Inventory is a current asset. An inventory write-down will have no effect on revenue and will decrease average working capital as any write-down to market value or net realizable value reduces the value of inventory, and the loss in value (expense) is generally reflected in the income statement in cost of goods sold. Therefore, the numerator is not affected and the denominator is reduced leading to a higher working capital turnover ratio."
